{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791029170,"nonce":"MJ/OlCbbypdXr17zv5XWtp8o5zU=","title":"Type builders and hlp_dyn_castp take the GC lock and HL_GLOBAL_LOCK in opposite orders","message":"`hl_get_obj_proto` and `hlp_get_obj_rt` (std/src/obj.rs) take `HL_GLOBAL_LOCK` and then the GC lock. `hlp_dyn_castp` (std/src/cast.rs) holds the GC lock for the whole cast, including the class's `__cast`, which is Haxe code; when that code allocates an object of a type not yet built it reaches the builders and takes `HL_GLOBAL_LOCK` second. Two worker threads building types meet in the middle and deadlock. The main thread then waits in `Lock.wait` forever, which is how it looks from Haxe.\n\nFound under ChimpMonk's parallel narrowphase (pb-wt.hl, see 9fac9a5), after the Deque lost wake-up was fixed: `ASH_WORKERS=4 ./pb-wt-now stacks2d 4` still hung about half its AOT runs. A trace ring on the two locks showed it: t4 `glock-got` then `gc-wait` with the owner being t3; t3 `gc-acq` then `glock-wait`. Samples: one worker `hlp_dyn_castp -\u003e ash_f56.__cast -\u003e hlp_alloc_obj_sized_flags -\u003e hl_get_obj_proto -\u003e hlp_get_obj_rt -\u003e Mutex::lock`, another `hl_get_obj_proto -\u003e ReentrantGcLock::acquire`.\n\nFix: the builders take the GC lock first. The other order (releasing the GC lock around Haxe callouts in hlp_dyn_castp) would also let other threads allocate while a `__cast` runs, but the cast keeps raw pointers into the source object across the call, so it needs its own analysis.","files":null}]}